  continue\\n            if success_count > best_success:\\n                best_success = success_count\\n                best_combo = combo\\n    result = [trials[idx]['reflection'] for idx in best_combo]\\n    return result\"}\n\nWe need to ensure no trailing spaces. Also the newlines are represented as \\n in the JSON string? Actually when we embed the string in JSON, we must escape actual newline characters as \\n. The above string contains literal newlines. In JSON, a string cannot contain unescaped newlines. So we must replace each newline with \\n. Similarly, if there were backslashes or double quotes, we'd escape. So we need to convert the multi-line string into a single line with \\n escapes.\n\nLet's do that. Write the code as a single line with \\n. Also note that we have double quotes inside? There's a chance that if we had a double quote in the string, we'd need \\\". But none. So we can produce:\n\n\"import itertools\\\\ndef solve(case):\\\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\\\n    trials = case['trials']\\\\n    n = len(trials)\\\\n    best_success = -1\\\\n    best_combo = None\\\\n    for size in range(0, capacity+1):\\\\n        for combo in itertools.combinations(range(n), size):\\\\n            sigs = set()\\\\n            ok = True\\\\n            success_count = 0\\\\n            for idx in combo:\\\\n                trial = trials[idx]\\\\n                sig = trial['signature']\\\\n                if sig in sigs:\\\\n                    ok = False\\\\n                    break\\\\n                sigs.add(sig)\\\\n                if trial['success']:\\\\n                    success_count += 1\\\\n            if not ok:\\\\n                continue\\\\n            if success_count > best_success:\\\\n                best_success = success_count\\\\n                best_combo = combo\\\\n    result = [trials[idx]['reflection'] for idx in best_combo]\\\\n    return result\"\n\nNote: In JSON, backslash is an escape character, so to represent a literal backslash, we need double backslash. Actually, in the Python source, we don't have any backslashes except the newline escapes. So we are replacing newlines with \\n (backslash n). In JSON, the backslash character itself must be escaped, so we write \\\\n. Wait: No, when we embed the string in JSON, we need to ensure that the string value has the proper escape sequences. The JSON string will contain backslash followed by n, which is interpreted as newline. So we must write the JSON string with a backslash and n. But in the Python source we are representing the newline as \\n. So in the JSON string, we must write \"\\\\n\" because the first backslash escapes the second for JSON. Let's clarify.\n\nWe have Python source code that contains newline characters. To put it in a JSON string, we need to represent each newline as the two-character sequence backslash and n. But when writing the JSON literal, we need to escape the backslash itself. So we write: \"\\\\n\" (in JSON source, that becomes a backslash and an n). So in the final JSON text (the output we are producing), we need to have double backslashes before n to represent a literal '\\n' in the string value.\n\nThus, for each newline in the Python source, we replace it with \"\\\\n\". Similarly, if the Python source contained a double quote, we'd replace with \\\" . But no.\n\nSo the correct JSON string value for the source code will have double backslashes where we want a newline.\n\nLet's write the source code as a single string with \\n representing newlines. For example: \"import itertools\\ndef solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    ...\" but in JSON we need to escape the backslashes. So we produce: \"import itertools\\\\ndef solve(case):\\\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\\\n    ...\"\n\nI'll carefully produce the entire string.\n\nI'll now write the final answer as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string.\n\nWe must ensure no extra whitespace or characters outside